// SRAM of a BLANK High Score Cart: the 317 bytes generateDefaultSram()
// overlays on a zeroed 2 KB (ORIG/JS7800/site/src/js/highscore.js). The
// remaining 1731 bytes of the 2048 stay zero.
//
// NOT the same as zeroing, and this cost a hardware round: the BIOS reads
// $10B3,X and takes 0x7F to mean "slot free" (LDA $10B3,X / CMP #$7F /
// BEQ, straight out of the dump). A zeroed table looks to it like 69
// OCCUPIED slots belonging to game id 0/0, so it shows a score of 0, shows
// no previous entries and cannot store a new one - exactly what Xevious,
// Asteroids and Joust did on hardware with 0.52/0.53.
//
// Layout, cross-checked against the BIOS's own indexing:
//   0x000-0x028  header, signature 68 83 AA 55 at offset 2
//   0x029-0x06D  69 entries, read as $1029,X
//   0x06E-0x0B2  69 entries, read as $106E,X
//   0x0B3-0x0F7  69 entries, read as $10B3,X - ALL 0x7F when free
//   0x0F8-0x13C  69 entries, all 0x7F
//   0x13D-0x7FF  the score area (JS7800's SRAM_SCORE_OFFSET $113D), zero
